## 1. Taxonomy Findings Summary (for prompt embedding)

### F1: Bridger Gateway Effect
> In the 22-case corpus, the **large majority** of propagation edges originate 
> from a Bridger source. Most of these start from root source nodes. 
> Government/official agencies account for the **largest share** of source platforms.
> These Bridgers provide the raw material that all subsequent actors modify.

### F2: Asymmetric Role Transitions
> - **Bridger → Amplifier** is the dominant transition (most frequent single edge type)
> - **Bridger → Bridger** is the second most common (legitimacy transfer between institutional actors)
> - **Bridger → Extender** is the third most common
> - Amplifier can chain to Amplifier, creating cumulative minor shifts that add up
> - Extender → Amplifier creates a two-step reframing chain (extend scope, then emphasize)
> - Adverser as target is **uncommon but not negligible** — it appears in a meaningful 
>   minority of cases, especially in polarized or controversial topics

### F3: Role-Operation Signatures
> - **Amplifiers** primarily use text-layer and visual-layer operations: 
>   `add annotation`, `change color`, `change title` — 
>   "change the packaging, not the data" (data-layer operations are infrequent)
> - **Extenders** have the highest data-layer proportion of any role: 
>   `add data variables` is their signature operation, combined with 
>   `change title` and `add annotation`
> - **Adversers** use selective data operations combined with source removal: 
>   `select time range`, `delete source`, `add annotation` — 
>   "reshape the evidence base" with cross-layer operations
> - **Bridgers** when modifying use mainly visual changes: color adjustments 
>   for platform consistency, minimal data manipulation

### F4: Cross-Platform Authority Gradient
> Platform tiers: T1 (Gov/Research) → T2 (News/3rd-party/Org Blog) → T3 (Social/UGC/Personal)
> - **Downward flow dominates**: edges tend to flow from higher to lower tiers
> - T2→T3 transfers are strongly associated with significant shifts
> - T1→T3 (two-tier drop): often produces significant shifts, including some 
>   of the strongest non-adverser shifts in the corpus
> - Same-tier transfers are **bimodal**: roughly split between minor and significant — 
>   role transition mediates shift within same tier
> - **Platform source-sink polarity**: Gov/Official is a strong net source, 
>   Social Media and Online Community are strong net sinks

### F5: Adverser Threshold
> **Major** (extreme) framing shifts are strongly associated with Adverser involvement. 
> Without adversarial reframing, shifts typically remain at minor-to-significant levels. 
> However, a well-positioned non-adverser actor with extensive cross-layer operations 
> can occasionally approach the boundary of major shift territory.

### F6: Operation Complexity Correlates with Shift Magnitude
> - Fewer operations (1 op) tend to produce minor or no shifts
> - Moderate operations (2 ops) lean toward significant shifts
> - Higher operation counts (3+ ops) are strongly associated with significant or major shifts
> - Single-layer operations tend toward minor/no shift
> - Multi-layer operations (2+ layers) lean heavily toward significant or major shifts
> - Three-layer operations (data+visual+text) almost always produce significant or major shifts
> - Major shifts generally involve high operation counts including data-layer manipulation

### F7: Topology Types
> - **Tree topology** is dominant: divergent, one source fans to multiple variants
> - **Linear topology** is less common: sequential chain, found in highly 
>   politicized or niche topics (e.g., immigration, IQ debates, housing, fossil fuels)

### F8: Platform Source-Sink Polarity
> - Gov/Official: strong net source
> - Research Inst: weak net source
> - News Media: moderate net sink, intermediary role
> - Social Media: strong net sink
> - Online Community: strong net sink
> - This reveals a structural asymmetry: T1 produces but rarely absorbs; 
>   T3 absorbs and modifies but rarely feeds back upstream

